  "title": "Product Marginals and Density-Moment Thresholds for Ising Transfer Kernels",
  "abstract": "Fix a positive Ising coupling. We construct positive Boolean weights with uniform labelled marginals up to linear order while exponentially many normalized transfer eigenvalues approach one; the Perron stationary law has the same marginals. Separate product-tilt constructions and hypercontractive bounds identify the density-moment boundary for a dimension-independent normalized transfer gap. At s=(1+rho)/(1-rho), the exact norm-budget boundary is 2^(1/s). Escaped critical moment below one suffices for a uniform gap, while one collapsing family satisfies every tail cap one at cutoffs at least one and total critical moment at most two. The same tail cap below cutoff one guarantees a positive gap. Stable spikes show that critical-power uniform integrability is not necessary. For (s-1)/s<K<1 we further construct collapse with density at least K and strict K-tail moment at most 1+C_s(1-K)K^(s-1)<2, where C_s=s^s/(s-1)^(s-1). The constructive budget is attained without strict floor or budget slack. The associated scalar background optimization is solved; the universal spectral threshold below cutoff one remains open. The code, tilt and spike families are distinct. Classical hypercontractivity, sign compression, product tilts and rank-one perturbations supply the mechanisms. External priority is unestablished. No fixed-range Gibbs, local-dynamics, physical-gap or continuum conclusion is claimed.",
